\title[Splitting labels are not residue filters]{Splitting Labels Are Not Residue Filters:\\
Non-Abelianness as an Obstruction to Candidate Narrowing}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{21 September 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A \emph{splitting-label battery} attached to a semiprime $N = pq$ measures, for a family of
small auxiliary polynomials $f_i$, the decomposition type of the hidden prime factors ---
concretely, the number of roots of $f_i$ modulo $p$. Such batteries have substantial measured
information content about the pair $(p,q)$; the configuration motivating this work reports a
joint capacity of $12.7$ bits. We settle the \emph{utility} question: whether that information
can be converted into candidate-set narrowing, i.e.\ into a sound rule that, on reading the
labels, excludes residue classes of $p$ modulo some $m$.

The answer is negative, and the obstruction is structural rather than quantitative. Working
with the smallest non-abelian probe $f(X) = X^3 - 2$, whose splitting field
$\Q(\sqrt[3]{2},\omega)$ has Galois group $S_3$, we prove: (i) an exact anatomy of the label
$T(p) = \#\{x \in \Z/p : x^3 \equiv 2\}$, namely $T(p) = 1 \iff p \equiv 2 \pmod 3$ and
$T(p) \in \{0,3\}$ otherwise; (ii) that for every modulus $2 \le m \le 24$ there is \emph{no}
function $g : \Z/m \to \mathbb{N}$ with $g(p \bmod m) = T(p)$ on the primes $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$,
each case refuted by an explicit pair of primes in one class carrying the labels $0$ and $3$,
with refutations propagating to all divisors; (iii) a \emph{no-pinning theorem}: every
\emph{sound} label filter must assign identical residue support to the labels $0$ and $3$ ---
at modulus $9$ all of $\{1,4,7\}$ is accepted by both, so the narrowing factor is exactly $1$.

We then give the matching information-theoretic layer for a finite (residue, label) channel:
the chain rule $H(R,T) = H(R) + H(T \mid R)$; the capacity law $I(R;T) \le H(T)$; saturation,
$I(R;T) = H(T)$ whenever the label is a function of the residue; and the gap theorem,
$I(R;T) < H(T)$ strictly whenever one residue class carries two labels of positive mass.
Consequently a measured deficit $I < H(T)$ --- such as the reported $I = 1.0012$ against
$H(T) = 2.2982$ --- is a \emph{certificate} that no residue-to-label table exists. Under
equidistribution the deficit is total: $I(R;T) = 0$ while $H(T) = \log 3 - \tfrac23\log 2 > 0$.
An adversarial control anchors the interpretation: for the abelian probe $X^2 - 2$ the table
does exist, at modulus $8$. Filterability is a statement about abelianness of the splitting
field, not about the number of bits available.
\end{abstract}

