\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The setting}
Let $N = pq$ be a semiprime with unknown prime factors. A \emph{dial} is an arithmetic
measurement returning a small invariant of the factors. The prototypical dial is built from a
monic integer polynomial $f$: its label at a prime $p$ is
\[
  T_f(p) \;=\; \#\{\, x \in \Z/p \;:\; f(x) \equiv 0 \pmod p \,\},
\]
which records the splitting type of $p$ in the number field defined by $f$. A \emph{battery} is
a finite family of dials; its readings form a label vector. The battery motivating this paper
has six dials and a measured joint capacity of $12.7$ bits with respect to the pair
$(p \bmod m^*, q \bmod m^*)$ for a fixed auxiliary modulus $m^*$, against a joint residue-vector
entropy of roughly $20$ bits.

\subsection{The utility question}
Searches over prime candidates are organized by residue classes: knowing $p \bmod m$ to lie in
$S \subseteq \Z/m$ prunes the pool by the factor $|S|/\varphi(m)$. So one asks for a lookup
table: read the label vector, consult a table indexed by labels, discard the forbidden classes.
Such a table is a \emph{filter}, and the demand it places on arithmetic is explicit: the label
must be a \emph{function of the residue},
\[
  \exists\, g : \Z/m \to \mathbb{N} \quad\text{with}\quad g(p \bmod m) = T_f(p)
  \ \text{ for all primes } p .
\]
Nothing weaker suffices for a table lookup, and --- as \S\ref{sec:refutation} shows --- nothing
weaker even in the one-sided, approximate sense suffices for narrowing.

\subsection{The design flaw, and its promotion to a theorem}
The experiment prompting this analysis built its utility tables by \emph{polynomial evaluation
at the residue}: it asked ``is $f(r) \equiv 0 \pmod{m^*}$?'' and used the answer as a proxy for
the label of a prime in the class $r$. This conflates a property of the integer $r$ with a
property of the primes lying in the class $r$. A built-in consistency assertion --- verifying
that the constructed filter never excludes the \emph{true} $p$ --- caught the discrepancy:
$6$ exclusions of the true prime in $150$ trials for one family of tables, while a second family
passed $0/150$ only because its accepted sets covered nearly all residues, i.e.\ only by not
filtering at all.

The diagnosis is the finding. The conversion requires a map
\[
  r \bmod m^* \;\longmapsto\; \text{splitting type of a prime } \equiv r \pmod{m^*},
\]
and \textbf{that map does not exist} for a non-abelian probe. Primes in the same residue class
carry different splitting types --- which is also exactly why every measured channel sits
strictly below its label-entropy ceiling: the gap \emph{is} the within-class variation.

\section{Definitions}

\begin{definition}[cube-root count]
For integers $c \ge 0$ and $p \ge 1$ set
$C(c,p) = \#\{x \in \{0,\dots,p-1\} : x^3 \equiv c \pmod p\}$. For prime $p$ this equals
$\#\{x \in \Z/p : x^3 = \bar c\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[the cubic label]
The \emph{cubic label} of $p$ is $T(p) = C(2,p)$, the number of cube roots of $2$ modulo $p$:
the label of the probe $f(X) = X^3 - 2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[quadratic label]
Set $S(c,p) = \#\{x \in \Z/p : x^2 = \bar c\}$; the \emph{quadratic label} of $p$ is $S(2,p)$,
the label of the probe $f(X) = X^2 - 2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[residue-to-type map]\label{def:table}
A modulus $m \ge 1$ \emph{admits a residue-to-type map} if there is $g : \Z/m \to \mathbb{N}$ with
\[
  g(p \bmod m) = T(p) \qquad\text{for every prime } p \ge 5 \text{ with } p \equiv 1 \!\!\pmod 3 .
\]
The restriction to $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$ is deliberate: by Theorem~\ref{thm:anatomy} the label on
the complementary classes is already a function of $p \bmod 3$, so including them would only
make the hypothesis easier to refute. We refute the strongest form.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[sound label filter]\label{def:sound}
A \emph{label filter} modulo $m$ is a map $F$ assigning to each observable label $t$ a set
$F(t) \subseteq \Z/m$ of permitted residues. It is \emph{sound} if it never excludes a true prime:
$(p \bmod m) \in F(T(p))$ for every prime $p \ge 5$. Soundness is the minimal correctness
requirement on a search-pruning rule: an unsound filter can delete the answer.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[finite residue--label channel]\label{def:channel}
Let $R, T$ be finite sets. A \emph{channel} is $P : R \times T \to \R_{\ge 0}$ with
$\sum_{r,t} P(r,t) = 1$; its marginals are $P_R(r) = \sum_t P(r,t)$ and $P_T(t) = \sum_r P(r,t)$.
With $\eta(x) = -x\log x$ and $\eta(0) = 0$,
\[
  H(R) = \sum_r \eta(P_R(r)),\quad H(T) = \sum_t \eta(P_T(t)),\quad H(R,T) = \sum_{r,t}\eta(P(r,t)),
\]
and, writing $\kappa(a,s) = -a\log a + a\log s$,
\[
  H(T \mid R) = \sum_{r}\sum_{t} \kappa\bigl(P(r,t),\,P_R(r)\bigr),
  \qquad I(R;T) = H(T) - H(T \mid R).
\]
\end{definition}

\section{The anatomy of the cubic label}

\subsection{The abelian half}

\begin{lemma}[inverting the cube map]\label{lem:inv}
Let $p$ be prime and suppose $3k = 2(p-1)+1$ for some integer $k \ge 0$. Then $(x^3)^k = x$ for
every $x \in \Z/p$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(x^3)^k = x^{2(p-1)+1}$. For $x = 0$ both sides vanish; for $x \ne 0$, Fermat's little theorem
gives $x^{p-1} = 1$, so $x^{2(p-1)+1} = (x^{p-1})^2 x = x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[cubing is bijective when $p \equiv 2 \bmod 3$]\label{thm:bij}
If $p$ is prime with $p \equiv 2 \pmod 3$, then $x \mapsto x^3$ is a bijection of $\Z/p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$2(p-1)+1 = 2p-1 \equiv 0 \pmod 3$, so $k = (2p-1)/3 \in \Z$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:inv} applies.
Injectivity: $x^3 = y^3 \Rightarrow x = (x^3)^k = (y^3)^k = y$. Surjectivity: $(y^k)^3 = (y^3)^k = y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[the abelian half is a filter]\label{cor:abelianhalf}
For every prime $p \equiv 2 \pmod 3$ and every $c$ we have $C(c,p) = 1$; in particular $T(p) = 1$.
\end{corollary}

On half the primes the label is constant, and that constancy is itself a residue condition. This
part of the label is a valid filter --- and a useless one, since it reports $p \bmod 3$, which
any residue-indexed search already knows.

\subsection{The non-abelian half}

\begin{lemma}[the group of cube roots of unity]\label{lem:mu3}
If $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$ then $\mu_3 = \{x \in \Z/p : x^3 = 1\}$ has exactly $3$ elements.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Upper bound: $\mu_3$ lies in the root set of $X^3 - 1$ over a field, of size at most $3$.
Lower bound: $3 \mid p-1 = |(\Z/p)^\times|$, so Cauchy's theorem supplies a unit $u$ of order
exactly $3$, and $1, u, u^2$ are three distinct cube roots of unity.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[fibres are cosets]\label{lem:coset}
Let $c \ne 0$ in $\Z/p$ with $a^3 = c$ for some $a$. Then $\{x : x^3 = c\} = a\,\mu_3$, so the
fibre has cardinality $|\mu_3|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
If $p$ is prime with $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$ and $p \nmid c$, then $C(c,p) \in \{0,3\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
An empty fibre gives $0$; a nonempty one gives $|\mu_3| = 3$ by Lemmas~\ref{lem:coset}
and~\ref{lem:mu3}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The exact information content}

\begin{theorem}[label anatomy]\label{thm:anatomy}
For every prime $p \ge 5$,
\[
  T(p) = 1 \iff p \equiv 2 \!\!\pmod 3,
  \qquad\text{and}\qquad
  p \equiv 1 \!\!\pmod 3 \implies T(p) \in \{0,3\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) is Corollary~\ref{cor:abelianhalf}. ($\Rightarrow$): a prime $p \ge 5$ is
$1$ or $2$ mod $3$; in the first case Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy} with $c = 2$ gives
$T(p) \in \{0,3\}$, excluding the value $1$.
\end{proof}

So the cubic label decomposes into a \emph{free bit} --- the value of $p \bmod 3$, already
available to a residue search --- and a \emph{hard bit}: on the classes $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$,
whether $2$ is a cube modulo $p$. Galois-theoretically, the free bit is the image of the
Frobenius class in the abelianization $S_3 \to S_3^{\ab} \cong \Z/2$, corresponding to the
cyclotomic subfield $\Q(\omega) \subseteq \Q(\sqrt[3]2,\omega)$. The hard bit lives past the
abelianization.

\section{The refutation}\label{sec:refutation}

\subsection{No residue-to-type map}

\begin{lemma}[divisor propagation]\label{lem:divprop}
If $d \mid m$ and $d$ admits a residue-to-type map, so does $m$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Compose the map for $d$ with the projection $\Z/m \to \Z/d$; since $p \bmod m$ projects to
$p \bmod d$, the composite computes $T(p)$.
\end{proof}

Contrapositively, a refutation at $m$ refutes every divisor of $m$, so the finite check below
rules out a much larger family of moduli than its literal range.

\begin{lemma}[witness refutation]\label{lem:witness}
If $a, b \ge 5$ are primes with $a \equiv b \equiv 1 \pmod 3$, $a \equiv b \pmod m$ and
$T(a) \ne T(b)$, then $m$ admits no residue-to-type map.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[the labels are not filters]\label{thm:notable}
For every modulus $m$ with $2 \le m \le 24$ there is no function $g : \Z/m \to \mathbb{N}$
satisfying $g(p \bmod m) = T(p)$ for all primes $p \ge 5$ with $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
One witness pair per modulus, each verified by direct computation of the two labels and of the
shared residue. Below, $a$ carries the label $0$, $b$ carries the label $3$, and
$a \equiv b \equiv r \pmod m$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cccc@{\hskip 2.2em}cccc}
\toprule
$m$ & $r$ & $a$ & $b$ & $m$ & $r$ & $a$ & $b$\\
\midrule
$2$ & $1$ & $7$ & $31$ & $14$ & $3$ & $73$ & $31$\\
$3$ & $1$ & $7$ & $31$ & $15$ & $13$ & $13$ & $43$\\
$4$ & $3$ & $7$ & $31$ & $16$ & $15$ & $79$ & $31$\\
$5$ & $3$ & $13$ & $43$ & $17$ & $7$ & $7$ & $109$\\
$6$ & $1$ & $7$ & $31$ & $18$ & $13$ & $13$ & $31$\\
$7$ & $3$ & $73$ & $31$ & $19$ & $13$ & $13$ & $127$\\
$8$ & $7$ & $7$ & $31$ & $20$ & $3$ & $103$ & $43$\\
$9$ & $4$ & $13$ & $31$ & $21$ & $10$ & $73$ & $31$\\
$10$ & $3$ & $13$ & $43$ & $22$ & $9$ & $97$ & $31$\\
$11$ & $9$ & $97$ & $31$ & $23$ & $19$ & $19$ & $157$\\
$12$ & $7$ & $7$ & $31$ & $24$ & $7$ & $7$ & $31$\\
$13$ & $1$ & $79$ & $157$ & & & &\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The cleanest single example is $(7,31)$: their difference is $24$, so they collide modulo every
divisor of $24$, and $T(7) = 0$ while $T(31) = 3$ because $4^3 = 64 \equiv 2 \pmod{31}$.
\end{proof}

The refutation is robust in a strong sense: because the two witnesses lie in a common class,
\emph{no} assignment of a single label to that class is correct for all primes in it.

\subsection{No pinning: sound filters do not narrow}

\begin{lemma}[shared classes are shared by both labels]\label{lem:shared}
Let $F$ be a sound filter mod $m$ and let $a, b \ge 5$ be primes with $a \equiv b \pmod m$,
$T(a) = 0$ and $T(b) = 3$. Then $(a \bmod m) \in F(0) \cap F(3)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[no pinning at modulus $9$]\label{thm:nopin9}
Every sound filter $F$ mod $9$ satisfies
$\{1,4,7\} \subseteq F(0)$ and $\{1,4,7\} \subseteq F(3)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:shared} once per class of $\Z/9$ congruent to $1$ mod $3$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ccc}
\toprule
class mod $9$ & prime with label $0$ & prime with label $3$\\
\midrule
$1$ & $19$ & $127$\\
$4$ & $13$ & $31$\\
$7$ & $7$ & $43$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Each row forces its class into both $F(0)$ and $F(3)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[narrowing factor exactly one]\label{cor:factor1}
For any sound filter $F$ mod $9$, $|F(0) \cap F(3)| \ge 3$: at least three residues survive both
readings of the hard bit. Since only three classes mod $9$ are congruent to $1$ mod $3$, the hard
bit removes nothing at all.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[the free bit, and only the free bit]\label{thm:freebit}
Every sound filter $F$ mod $9$ satisfies $\{2,5,8\} \subseteq F(1)$, and by
Theorem~\ref{thm:anatomy} the label $1$ occurs only at primes $\equiv 2 \bmod 3$. Hence the only
separation a sound filter can effect is $\{2,5,8\}$ versus $\{1,4,7\}$, i.e.\ the value of
$p \bmod 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The primes $11, 5, 17$ have label $1$ and reduce to $2, 5, 8$ mod $9$; soundness places each in
$F(1)$. The converse is Theorem~\ref{thm:anatomy}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[no pinning at every modulus $\le 24$]\label{thm:nopin}
For every $m$ with $2 \le m \le 24$ and every sound filter $F$ mod $m$ there is a residue class
$r \in \Z/m$ with $r \in F(0) \cap F(3)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take the witness pair of Theorem~\ref{thm:notable} and apply Lemma~\ref{lem:shared}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[the empirical picture is even flatter]
Theorem~\ref{thm:nopin} guarantees one shared class per modulus. Enumeration of the primes below
$5000$ shows far more: for every $2 \le m \le 24$ the minimal sound accept-sets satisfy
$F_{\min}(0) = F_{\min}(3)$ on the classes coprime to $m$, so the ratio
$|F_{\min}(0) \cap F_{\min}(3)| / |F_{\min}(0) \cup F_{\min}(3)|$ equals $1$ at every modulus.
(The class $0 \bmod m$ is excluded as degenerate: it contains the single prime $p = m$.)
\end{remark}

Whatever the dial reads, that class survives. A narrowing rule would have to say, at least
sometimes, ``not this class'' --- and it never can. Producing the narrowing would require
deciding the splitting type of the individual candidate prime, which is exactly the per-prime
determination the factoring problem withholds. The argument closes in a circle; that circle is
the theorem.

\section{The information layer: capacity, saturation, and the gap}

Fix a channel $P$ on $R \times T$ as in Definition~\ref{def:channel}.

\begin{lemma}[pointwise nonnegativity and strict positivity]\label{lem:kappa}
For $0 \le a \le s$ we have $\kappa(a,s) = -a\log a + a\log s \ge 0$, with $\kappa(a,a) = 0$ and
$\kappa(0,s) = 0$; and if $0 < a < s$ then $\kappa(a,s) > 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $a > 0$, $\kappa(a,s) = a(\log s - \log a)$, and $\log$ is strictly increasing on positives.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[marginal dominance]\label{lem:dom}
$P(r,t) \le P_R(r)$ for all $r, t$, the marginal being a sum of nonnegative terms including
$P(r,t)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[chain rule]\label{thm:chain}
$H(R,T) = H(R) + H(T \mid R)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $H(T\mid R) = \sum_{r,t}\bigl(\eta(P(r,t)) + P(r,t)\log P_R(r)\bigr)$. The first block is
$H(R,T)$; summing the second over $t$ for fixed $r$ gives $P_R(r)\log P_R(r) = -\eta(P_R(r))$,
and summing over $r$ gives $-H(R)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$I(R;T) = H(R) + H(T) - H(R,T)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[capacity law]\label{thm:capacity}
$H(T \mid R) \ge 0$, hence $I(R;T) \le H(T)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Termwise from Lemmas~\ref{lem:kappa} and~\ref{lem:dom}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[filters saturate the ceiling]\label{thm:saturate}
If the label is a function of the residue --- there is $g : R \to T$ with
$P(r,t) \ne 0 \Rightarrow t = g(r)$ --- then $H(T\mid R) = 0$ and $I(R;T) = H(T)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $P(r,t) \ne 0$ we have $t = g(r)$ and $P(r,t') = 0$ for all $t' \ne t$, so
$P_R(r) = P(r,t)$ and the term is $\kappa(a,a) = 0$; the remaining terms vanish because $a = 0$.
\end{proof}

Thus the utility programme's hypothesis is an information-theoretic hypothesis: a
residue-to-type table exists if and only if the channel attains its ceiling.

\begin{theorem}[the gap theorem]\label{thm:gap}
If there are $r \in R$ and $t_1 \ne t_2$ in $T$ with $P(r,t_1) > 0$ and $P(r,t_2) > 0$, then
$H(T\mid R) > 0$ and hence $I(R;T) < H(T)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$P(r,t_1) + P(r,t_2) \le P_R(r)$ and $P(r,t_2) > 0$ give $0 < P(r,t_1) < P_R(r)$, so that term is
strictly positive by Lemma~\ref{lem:kappa}, while all others are nonnegative.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[measured gaps are certificates]
A measured strict inequality $I(R;T) < H(T)$ is equivalent, by the contrapositive of
Theorem~\ref{thm:saturate}, to the existence of a residue class carrying two labels. The deficit
$H(T) - I(R;T) = H(T\mid R)$ \emph{is} the within-class variation; it is never attributable to
measurement error.
\end{corollary}

Applied to the reported reading $I = 1.0012$ against $H(T) = 2.2982$: the deficit of $1.297$ bits
is exactly the label entropy living inside residue classes, unavailable to any residue filter.

\begin{theorem}[independence: zero utility at positive entropy]\label{thm:indep}
If $P(r,t) = a(r)b(t)$ for probability vectors $a, b$, then $I(R;T) = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The marginals are $a$ and $b$; using $\eta(xy) = y\,\eta(x) + x\,\eta(y)$ one gets
$H(R,T) = H(R) + H(T)$, whence $I = 0$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The equidistribution model of the cubic channel}

Chebotarev-type equidistribution predicts that among primes in a fixed class mod $9$ congruent
to $1$ mod $3$, the cubic label is $0$ with density $2/3$ and $3$ with density $1/3$,
independently of the class. Encode this on $R = \{1,4,7\}$, $T = \{0,3\}$ by
\[
  P(r,t) = \tfrac13 \cdot
  \begin{cases} 2/3, & t = 0,\\ 1/3, & t = 3.\end{cases}
\]

\begin{theorem}[total gap in the equidistribution model]\label{thm:cheb}
$I(R;T) = 0$ and $H(T) = \log 3 - \tfrac23\log 2 > 0$, hence $I(R;T) < H(T)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The law is a product, so $I = 0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:indep}. The label marginal is $(2/3,1/3)$,
giving $H(T) = -\tfrac23\log\tfrac23 - \tfrac13\log\tfrac13 = \log 3 - \tfrac23\log 2$;
positivity is $3\log 3 > 2\log 2$, i.e.\ $27 > 4$.
\end{proof}

So in the idealized model the \emph{entire} label entropy is within-class variation, and the
channel's utility for residue narrowing is exactly zero. This is the Bayesian form of the
corrected understanding: the labels are statistics of the joint draw
$(p \bmod m^*, q \bmod m^*)$, and while the label vector is a genuine $12.7$-bit posterior update
on that $\approx 20$-bit joint vector, it is not an update on any single residue coordinate.

\begin{theorem}[no pinning, Bayesian form]\label{thm:nopinbayes}
In the equidistribution model, $P_R(r) = 1/3$ for every $r$ and $P(r,t) \le 1/3$ for every
$(r,t)$: the posterior mass of a single residue class is unchanged by the label, remaining at its
prior value. For the joint pair $(p \bmod 9, q \bmod 9)$ under the corresponding product model
the constant is $1/9$.
\end{theorem}

The principle: constant-bounded posterior mass on joint residue vectors, and no candidate filter
without circularity.

\section{The adversarial control: abelian probes \emph{are} filters}

\begin{theorem}[two square roots or none]\label{thm:sq}
For an odd prime $p$: if $2$ is a square in $\Z/p$ then $S(2,p) = 2$; otherwise $S(2,p) = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[the quadratic label is a residue function]\label{thm:sqtable}
For every odd prime $p$,
\[
  S(2,p) =
  \begin{cases}
    2, & p \equiv 1 \text{ or } 7 \pmod 8,\\
    0, & p \equiv 3 \text{ or } 5 \pmod 8,
  \end{cases}
\]
so the explicit map $g : \Z/8 \to \mathbb{N}$ with $g(r) = 2$ for $r \in \{1,7\}$ and $g(r) = 0$
otherwise satisfies $g(p \bmod 8) = S(2,p)$ for all odd primes $p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The second supplement to quadratic reciprocity: $2$ is a quadratic residue mod $p$ exactly when
$p \equiv \pm 1 \pmod 8$. Combine with Theorem~\ref{thm:sq}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[the boundary]\label{thm:boundary}
The abelian probe $X^2 - 2$ admits a residue-to-label table at modulus $8$; the non-abelian probe
$X^3 - 2$ admits no residue-to-type table at any modulus $2 \le m \le 24$.
\end{theorem}

The dividing line is the Galois group of the splitting field, not the size of the label alphabet
nor the number of available bits. $\Q(\sqrt2)$ is abelian over $\Q$ and embeds in $\Q(\zeta_8)$;
by Kronecker--Weber abelian extensions are exactly those whose splitting behaviour is governed by
congruence conditions, so a table must exist, and it does. By contrast
$\Gal(\Q(\sqrt[3]2,\omega)/\Q) \cong S_3$ has nontrivial commutator subgroup $A_3$; the label is
a class function of Frobenius, and its non-abelian part is invisible to every congruence. Only
the abelian quotient --- the $\Z/2$ recording $p \bmod 3$ --- filters. This is why adding more
non-abelian dials cannot help: more capacity for the joint draw, zero narrowing power for a
single residue coordinate.

\section{The measured window}

Consider the primes $5 \le p < 200$ with $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$, i.e.\ those whose label is not
already determined by $p \bmod 3$. There are $21$ of them. Classified by residue mod $9$
(necessarily $1$, $4$ or $7$) and by label ($0$ or $3$):

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|cc|c}
\toprule
$p \bmod 9$ & $T(p) = 0$ & $T(p) = 3$ & row total\\
\midrule
$1$ & $6$ & $2$ & $8$\\
$4$ & $5$ & $2$ & $7$\\
$7$ & $5$ & $1$ & $6$\\
\midrule
total & $16$ & $5$ & $21$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{theorem}[census]\label{thm:census}
The window contains exactly $21$ primes, with the cell counts displayed above; every prime of the
window has label $0$ or $3$ (an instance of Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy}); and all six cells are
strictly positive, so every residue class carries both labels.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[the measured channel gap]
For the empirical channel $P(r,t) = (\text{cell count})/21$, Theorem~\ref{thm:gap} applies with
$r$ the class $1$ and $t_1 = 0 \ne t_2 = 3$, so $H(T\mid R) > 0$ and $I(R;T) < H(T)$ strictly ---
the qualitative shape of every dial reading, including $I = 1.0012 < 2.2982 = H(T)$.
\end{corollary}

The census is not what makes the verdict general: that is Theorems~\ref{thm:notable}
and~\ref{thm:nopin}. The census witnesses that the phenomenon is visible in a tiny window and
supplies the numbers whose entropy deficit matches the measured gap.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Label evaluation}
For a prime $p \ge 5$: if $p \equiv 2 \pmod 3$ return $1$. Otherwise apply Euler's criterion for
cubic residues --- $2$ is a cube modulo $p$ iff $2^{(p-1)/3} \equiv 1 \pmod p$ --- and return $3$
or $0$ accordingly. Cost $O(\log p)$ modular multiplications, against $O(p)$ for enumeration.
Correctness: the cubic residues are the kernel of $x \mapsto x^{(p-1)/3}$, an index-$3$ subgroup.

\subsection{Witness search}
Given a modulus $m$ and bound $B$, sweep the primes $p \le B$ with $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$, bucket by
$(p \bmod m, T(p))$, and emit the first class containing both labels. Cost $\tilde O(B)$.
Empirically the smallest witness for every $m \le 24$ occurs below $B = 200$.

\subsection{Sound-filter maximality check}
Compute the minimal sound accept-sets
$F_{\min}(t) = \{p \bmod m : T(p) = t,\ p \le B,\ \gcd(p,m) = 1\}$ and the narrowing factor
$\nu(m) = |F_{\min}(0) \cap F_{\min}(3)| / |F_{\min}(0) \cup F_{\min}(3)|$. Any sound filter
contains $F_{\min}$, so $\nu(m) = 1$ certifies that no sound filter narrows. The computation
returns $\nu(m) = 1$ for every $m \le 24$.

\subsection{Channel statistics}
From the cell counts compute $H(R), H(T), H(R,T)$ by summation of $\eta$, then
$H(T\mid R) = H(R,T) - H(R)$ and $I = H(T) - H(T\mid R)$, at cost $O(|R||T|)$. The diagnostic
quantity is the gap $H(T) - I = H(T\mid R)$.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What was refuted, and what was not}
We did not refute that splitting labels carry information --- they do, provably and measurably.
We refuted the conversion of that information into candidate narrowing via residue classes. A
channel may have high capacity with respect to one random variable and zero with respect to
another; the utility of a measurement is always relative to the variable one intends to act on.

\subsection{Capacity is about the joint draw}
The labels are statistics of the pair $(p \bmod m^*, q \bmod m^*)$; their $12.7$ bits are a
genuine posterior update on that joint vector, and the capacity law $I \le H(T)$ holds with room
to spare. But an update on a joint law is not automatically an update on a marginal, and it is
the marginal that a sieve can act on. The gap theorem quantifies the difference.

\subsection{Why non-abelianness is the right invariant}
Kronecker--Weber says a finite abelian extension of $\Q$ lies in a cyclotomic field; equivalently
its splitting behaviour is determined by a congruence on $p$ --- a residue-to-type table with
modulus the conductor. Conversely, a table for a probe with splitting field $L$ forces the
Frobenius class in $\Gal(L/\Q)$ to be a function of $p \bmod m$, hence $L \subseteq \Q(\zeta_m)$,
hence $\Gal(L/\Q)$ abelian. For $L = \Q(\sqrt[3]2,\omega)$ it is not, and $\Q(\sqrt[3]2)$ is not
even normal over $\Q$. The finite verification of \S\ref{sec:refutation} is an unconditional
shadow of this structural fact: it presupposes neither Chebotarev nor class field theory, it
merely exhibits collisions.

\subsection{The circularity}
Reading the hard bit for a specific candidate $p$ requires deciding whether $2$ is a cube modulo
$p$: cheap if $p$ is known, impossible if $p$ is what one is hunting. The battery reads labels of
the hidden factors; using them as a filter would require comparing against per-candidate labels,
but candidates are residue classes, and a class has no label. Any attempt to give it one is
refuted by Theorem~\ref{thm:notable}.

\subsection{Methodological note}
The finding began as a bug: utility tables built by evaluating the probe at the residue rather
than by determining the type of primes in that class. What converted a bug into a theorem was a
soundness assertion --- a runtime check that the constructed filter never excludes the true
factor. The check failed, and the failure had a diagnosis of much wider scope than the code path
that produced it. In any pipeline that prunes a search space, instrument the pruning step with a
``never excludes the truth'' assertion on instances where the truth is known.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[unconditional non-existence]
For every modulus $m \ge 1$ there exist primes $p \equiv q \pmod m$, both $\equiv 1 \pmod 3$, with
$\#\{x : x^3 \equiv 2 \bmod p\} = 0$ and $\#\{x : x^3 \equiv 2 \bmod q\} = 3$. Equivalently, the
cubic label is not periodic in $p$ under any modulus.
\end{conjecture}

Periodicity of a splitting label modulo $m$ is exactly the statement that the splitting field
embeds in $\Q(\zeta_m)$, i.e.\ that the label's field is abelian --- and $\Q(\sqrt[3]2,\omega)$
has Galois group $S_3$ with nontrivial commutator subgroup. A proof needs only the abelian half
of class field theory plus the explicit $S_3$ structure, not full Chebotarev: a period $m$ forces
$\Q(\sqrt[3]2) \subseteq \Q(\zeta_m)$, contradicted by comparing the ramification of $3$ or by the
non-normality of $\Q(\sqrt[3]2)$. The finite verification ($m \le 24$) is complete; the missing
step is a single structural lemma.

\begin{conjecture}[capacity--utility dichotomy]
For any finite family of dials whose labels are class functions of the Frobenius of a
non-abelian extension, the mutual information with the residue vector $p \bmod m$ is bounded by
the information carried by the maximal abelian subextension --- and that bound is the entire
utility: candidate narrowing beyond it is impossible.
\end{conjecture}

The capacity law $I \le H(T)$ should refine to $I \le H(T \text{ restricted to the
abelianization})$, because the residue vector is measurable with respect to the abelian quotient
only. In the channel framework of this paper this is a data-processing inequality for the
projection $\Gal(L/\Q) \to \Gal(L/\Q)^{\ab}$; the chain rule and its equality case are already
available, and the missing ingredient is a short data-processing lemma in the same pointwise
calculus.

\medskip

\noindent\textbf{Bayesian no-pinning.} Strengthen Theorem~\ref{thm:nopinbayes} from the product
model to the general case: for any battery of non-abelian dials, the posterior mass of any single
joint residue vector should remain bounded by a constant multiple of its prior, uniformly in the
label reading. This would convert ``the narrowing factor is $1$'' from a statement about sound
set-valued filters into one about arbitrary Bayesian decision rules, closing the last loophole:
soft, probability-weighted candidate reordering.

\medskip

\noindent\textbf{Higher-degree and composite probes.} Extend the census and refutations to
$X^n - a$ for $n = 4,5,6$ and to non-cyclotomic quartics, and locate the exact boundary between
probes with abelian splitting fields (tables exist), solvable-but-non-abelian ones (tables only
for the abelianization), and the rest.

\medskip

\noindent\textbf{Quantifying the useful fraction.} For a probe with splitting field $L$ the
abelianization captures a computable fraction of $H(T)$. Determine this fraction for standard
probe families and compare it with total battery capacity; the prediction is that the useful
fraction equals the abelian fraction exactly --- for $X^3 - 2$, one free bit and nothing else.

\section{Conclusion}

The battery measures honestly and measures a great deal. The measurement is about the joint
residue draw of the hidden factors; it is not about any single factor's residue class, and the
two are not interchangeable. For the smallest non-abelian probe this is now precise: the cubic
label splits into a free bit given by $p \bmod 3$ and a hard bit that is provably not a function
of $p \bmod m$ for any $m \le 24$; every sound residue filter must accept the same classes for
both readings of the hard bit, so the narrowing factor is exactly $1$; and the observed shortfall
$I < H(T)$ is not noise but a certificate of precisely that non-functionality, since $I = H(T)$
holds if and only if the label is a function of the residue. The control fixes the
interpretation: the abelian probe $X^2 - 2$ does yield a filter, at modulus $8$. What blocks the
cubic probe is not a shortage of bits but the non-abelianness of $S_3$ --- the part of the
Frobenius that no congruence can see. The labels are not filters.

